<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oenotrians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oenotrians were an ancient Italic people of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, recorded by Greek writers as living in what are now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilicata">Basilicata</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a> before the founding of the Greek colonies in the 8th century BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> called the region Oenotria, and the name Italia first referred to part of their land. They were absorbed by the Greek cities and later by the Lucanians and Bruttians.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oenotrians.jpg" alt="Oenotrians" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Museum of Brettii and Enotri · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Museo_Brettii_Enotri_-_Ricco_corredo_funerario_degli_Enotri,_preveniente_dalla_necropoli_di_Torre_del_Mordillo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Oenotrian is likely derived from the Greek oînos, wine, since the Oenotrians lived in a land rich in vineyards; Hesychius records the word oínōtron, a kind of vine stake. Their territory included western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilicata">Basilicata</a>, the Vallo di Diano, the coastal strip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilento">Cilento</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>, and the name Oenotria came to be extended to the whole of Southern Italy. They are thought to have been the southern branch of an ancient people, distinct from the Proto-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latins</a>, who lived along the Tyrrhenian coast from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liguria">Liguria</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, and ancient writers generally counted them among the Pelasgians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Greek tradition gave them a Greek ancestry. Ancient authors of the 1st century BCE said that Oenotria was named after Oenotrus, youngest of the fifty sons of Lycaon, who came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadia">Arcadia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, and according to Antoninus Liberalis and Hellanicus their arrival drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elymians">Elymians</a> to migrate to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. Virgil describes them as the settlers whose descendants called their land <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> after their leader Italus, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> saw in the islands of Pontia and Isacia, called the Oenotrides, proof that Italy was once held by the Oenotrians.</p><p>The first lasting Greek colonies, such as Metapontum, pushed them inland and began a war of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attrition">attrition</a> in which the Oenotrians plundered the Greek cities more than once. In time they disappeared under the pressure of the Lucanians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Whatever the Greek legends said of their origin, inscriptions found in 1991 at the Oenotrian settlement of Tortora in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>, dating from the 6th or the 5th century BCE, show that the Oenotrians spoke an Italic language.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oenotrians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They absorbed or displaced earlier peoples, the <b>Oenotrians</b> and Chones, and pressed the Greek cities of the coast, capturing Poseidonia around 410 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/lucanians">Lucanians</a></li></ul></section>
